ONE SERVER, ONE CLUSTER, NO DEFAULTS.

`crdb` is the only entry, and `entrypoint.sh` passes `--strict-mcp-config` so no other
MCP configuration on the image or in a home directory can add a second one.

`mcp-cluster-id` is the documented header that pins exactly one cluster; a tool call
naming a different cluster fails at the server. `Authorization` carries a Cluster
Operator service-account key scoped to `mcp:read` plus INSERT on
`mainline_meas.external_attestation` and nothing else (S13) — the SQL grant is the real
boundary and `settings.json`'s allowlist is written to match it exactly.

NEITHER VARIABLE CARRIES A `:-default`, DELIBERATELY. Claude Code substitutes `${VAR}` in
`url` and `headers`, and an unset variable with no default is left unexpanded and reported
as a missing-variable warning — the server then fails to connect. That is the outcome we
want: a Steward that cannot prove which cluster it is pinned to must not read one. A
default would turn a missing secret into a silent connection to whatever the default named.

The key is delivered by AWS Secrets Manager into the task environment. It is never baked
into the image, and `settings.json` denies `Read(**/.env)` so the session cannot read a
credential file even if one were mounted.
